  "id": "american_moravian_music",
  "name": "American Moravian music (Salem and Bethlehem trombone choirs and anthems)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Sacred and instrumental music of the Moravian Church settlements founded in the 18th century at Bethlehem and Nazareth, Pennsylvania, and Salem (now Winston-Salem), North Carolina, whose collegia musica performed European concert music and whose own composers, among them Johann Friedrich Peter and Jeremiah Dencke, wrote German-texted anthems and arias for voices with strings, winds and organ. Trombone choirs spanning soprano to bass instruments announce deaths, play chorales for church festivals and lead the Easter sunrise service, a practice that continues; the Moravian Music Foundation in Winston-Salem holds the manuscript archives. The fiddle card stands for the violins.",
  "recipe": "classic american moravian music, art Music, western common practice branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. yellow brass canonical 70 30 bach 42 standard bach 42 canonical American orchestral tenor legato tenor standard standard backbore trombone, mixed satb canonical 4 part western sacred liturgical global choir ensemble, fiddle, norway spruce European alpine European maple sycamore maple oil varnish cello, silbermann saxon canonical central German Baroque early 18th century tin lead 75 25 canonical principal rank bright projecting legato pipe organ. country church. small-diaphragm pencil condenser, transformerless solid-state pre. 24-bit digital.",
